  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066605/assets/431ba020bfc39615e1d49a866c6ee7c30cf8cc8f84ebde6866ef0e0cb9c8708b.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Determine the **expanded form** of $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $3.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,81$",
  "worked_solution": "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-3,$ ${x}_{1}=1,$ and ${x}_{3,4}=\\pm 3\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=3.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =3(x-(-3))(x-1)(x-(-3\\text{i}))(x-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =3(x+3)(x-1)(x+3\\text{i})(x-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+3)(x-1)(x+3\\text{i})(x-3\\text{i}) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-x+3x-3)({x}^{2}-3\\text{i}x+3\\text{i}x-(3\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+2x-3)({x}^{2}+9) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{4}+9{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3}+18x-3{x}^{2}-27) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+18x-27) & = \\\\ 3{x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}+54x-81 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}+54x\\,-81.$",
